1. What is polymorphism in Java? Explain with a real-life example. 
    Polymorphism means “many forms.” In Java, it allows objects to be treated 
as instances of their parent class rather than their actual class. 
It enables one interface to be used for different underlying forms (data types).
	Polymorphism allows methods to do different things based on the object that 
invokes them —even if they share the same name.
	Imagine a smartphone charger. You plug it into different phones—Samsung,
iPhone, Pixel—but it adapts and charges each one correctly. The charger is 
the common interface, and each phone is a different implementation.
	

2. Differentiate between compile-time and run-time polymorphism with examples.

compile time:
	Method Overloading
	Compile Time
	Same method name, different parameters
	add(int a,int b) , add(double a,double b)
	Utility methods, calculations
	
run time:
	Method Overriding
	Runtime
	Same method name, same parameters
	sound() overridden in Dog, Cat classes
	Dynamic behavior based on object type
	

	